      "story": {
        "kind": "Panel",
        "spec": {
          "data": {
            "kind": "QueryGroup",
            "spec": {
              "queries": [],
              "queryOptions": {},
              "transformations": []
            }
          },
          "description": "",
          "id": 8,
          "links": [],
          "title": "",
          "transparent": true,
          "vizConfig": {
            "group": "text",
            "kind": "VizConfig",
            "spec": {
              "fieldConfig": {
                "defaults": {},
                "overrides": []
              },
              "options": {
                "code": {
                  "language": "plaintext",
                  "showLineNumbers": false,
                  "showMiniMap": false
                },
                "content": "## Every flat map lies\n\nA globe cannot be flattened without tearing or stretching it. Every map projection picks what to keep: **areas**,\n**angles**, **distances** or **directions**, never all of them.\n\n**Mercator** keeps angles, which made it the sailors' map, and it is the projection of nearly every web map. The price\nis area: the farther from the equator, the larger it draws the land. On a Mercator map **Greenland takes more room\nthan Africa** (35 against 34 million km² of map); on the ground it is **14 times smaller** (2.2 against 29.9 million\nkm²). Ecuador, on the equator, comes out at its true size.\n\nSwitch projections above and watch Tissot's circles: all are 1,000 km across on the ground. Click a country, or drag\nacross latitudes, to compare its real area with Mercator's.\n",
                "mode": "markdown"
              }
            },
            "version": "13.3.0"
          }
        }
      },
